Below are the columns that would be used, and why they matter, followed by ten exploratory / building‑block SQL queries (each limited to 20 rows when they return tabular results).  
All queries only touch the tables listed in `mitelman-db.prod`.

Relevant columns and typical conditions  
• `Cytogen.RefNo` / `Cytogen.CaseNo`          – primary case identifiers  
• `Cytogen.Morph` = '3111'                    – ductal / invasive breast‑cancer morphology code  
• `Cytogen.Topo`  = '0401'                    – breast topography code  
• `CytoConverted.RefNo` / `CytoConverted.CaseNo` – link aberrations to cases  
• `CytoConverted.Chr`                         – chromosome (e.g. ‘chr1’)  
• `CytoConverted.Type`                        – ‘Gain’ | ‘Loss’ | etc.  
• `RecurrentNumData.Chromosome`               – 1‑22/X/Y (no ‘chr’ prefix)  
• `RecurrentNumData.Abnormality`              – ‘+1’, ‘‑22’, … (used to separate gains & losses)  
• `RecurrentNumData.Morph`, `Topo`            – same filters as above  
• Aggregate conditions                        – `HAVING COUNT(*) >= 5` to keep ≥ 5 matched records  

Ten incrementally more complex queries
------------------------------------------------

```sql
-- 1) Description: Show the 20 most‑common morphology codes in Cytogen.
SELECT Morph, COUNT(*) AS case_count
FROM `mitelman-db.prod.Cytogen`
GROUP BY Morph
ORDER BY case_count DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2) Description: Breast‑cancer (Morph = 3111) – list topographies and counts.
SELECT Topo, COUNT(*) AS case_count
FROM `mitelman-db.prod.Cytogen`
WHERE Morph = '3111'
GROUP BY Topo
ORDER BY case_count DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3) Description: Number of distinct breast‑cancer cases (Morph 3111, Topo 0401).
SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT CONCAT(CAST(RefNo AS STRING),'-',CaseNo)) AS breast_case_cnt
FROM `mitelman-db.prod.Cytogen`
WHERE Morph = '3111' AND Topo = '0401';
```

```sql
-- 4) Description: Sample 20 breast‑cancer investigations with short karyotypes.
SELECT c.RefNo, c.CaseNo, ci.InvNo, ci.KaryShort
FROM   `mitelman-db.prod.Cytogen`     AS c
JOIN   `mitelman-db.prod.CytogenInv`  AS ci
ON     c.RefNo = ci.RefNo AND c.CaseNo = ci.CaseNo
WHERE  c.Morph = '3111' AND c.Topo = '0401'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5) Description: Breast‑cancer CytoConverted rows (aberration list sample).
SELECT cc.RefNo, cc.CaseNo, cc.Chr, cc.Type
FROM   `mitelman-db.prod.CytoConverted` AS cc
WHERE  EXISTS (
         SELECT 1
         FROM   `mitelman-db.prod.Cytogen` AS c
         WHERE  c.RefNo = cc.RefNo
           AND  c.CaseNo = cc.CaseNo
           AND  c.Morph = '3111'
           AND  c.Topo  = '0401')
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6) Description: Count gains / losses per chromosome for breast‑cancer cases.
WITH breast_cases AS (
  SELECT RefNo, CaseNo
  FROM   `mitelman-db.prod.Cytogen`
  WHERE  Morph = '3111' AND Topo = '0401'
),
cyto AS (
  SELECT cc.Chr, cc.Type
  FROM   `mitelman-db.prod.CytoConverted` AS cc
  JOIN   breast_cases USING (RefNo, CaseNo)
)
SELECT Chr, Type, COUNT(*) AS aberration_count
FROM   cyto
GROUP  BY Chr, Type
ORDER  BY Chr, Type
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7) Description: Per‑chromosome gain frequency (only Chr with ≥5 gain cases shown).
WITH breast_cases AS (
  SELECT DISTINCT RefNo, CaseNo
  FROM   `mitelman-db.prod.Cytogen`
  WHERE  Morph = '3111' AND Topo = '0401'
),
total_cases AS (
  SELECT COUNT(*) AS n_cases FROM breast_cases
),
gains AS (
  SELECT cc.RefNo, cc.CaseNo, cc.Chr
  FROM   `mitelman-db.prod.CytoConverted` AS cc
  JOIN   breast_cases USING (RefNo, CaseNo)
  WHERE  cc.Type = 'Gain'
  GROUP  BY cc.RefNo, cc.CaseNo, cc.Chr      -- one vote per case / chr
),
gain_counts AS (
  SELECT Chr, COUNT(*) AS gain_case_cnt
  FROM   gains
  GROUP  BY Chr
)
SELECT g.Chr,
       gain_case_cnt,
       gain_case_cnt / t.n_cases AS gain_freq
FROM   gain_counts AS g CROSS JOIN total_cases AS t
WHERE  gain_case_cnt >= 5
ORDER  BY gain_freq DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8) Description: RecurrentNumData – raw abnormality counts for breast cancer.
SELECT Chromosome,
       Abnormality,
       SUM(CAST(TotalCases AS INT64)) AS total_cases
FROM   `mitelman-db.prod.RecurrentNumData`
WHERE  Morph = '3111' AND Topo = '0401'
GROUP  BY Chromosome, Abnormality
ORDER  BY Chromosome, Abnormality
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9) Description: Aggregate RecurrentNumData into Gain / Loss buckets (≥5 only).
WITH agg AS (
  SELECT Chromosome,
         CASE 
           WHEN STARTS_WITH(Abnormality,'+') THEN 'Gain'
           WHEN STARTS_WITH(Abnormality,'-') THEN 'Loss'
           ELSE 'Other'
         END AS AberrationType,
         SUM(CAST(TotalCases AS INT64)) AS cases
  FROM   `mitelman-db.prod.RecurrentNumData`
  WHERE  Morph = '3111' AND Topo = '0401'
  GROUP  BY Chromosome, AberrationType
)
SELECT Chromosome, AberrationType, cases
FROM   agg
WHERE  cases >= 5
ORDER  BY Chromosome, AberrationType
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
--10) Description: Example intra‑Mitelman Pearson correlation:
--      gain counts (CytoConverted) vs loss counts (RecurrentNumData).
WITH breast_cases AS (
  SELECT DISTINCT RefNo, CaseNo
  FROM   `mitelman-db.prod.Cytogen`
  WHERE  Morph = '3111' AND Topo = '0401'
),
cyto_gain AS (
  SELECT REPLACE(cc.Chr,'chr','') AS Chromosome
  FROM   `mitelman-db.prod.CytoConverted` AS cc
  JOIN   breast_cases USING (RefNo, CaseNo)
  WHERE  cc.Type = 'Gain'
  GROUP  BY RefNo, CaseNo, Chromosome      -- 1 per case/chr
),
gain_cnt AS (
  SELECT Chromosome, COUNT(*) AS gain_cases
  FROM   cyto_gain
  GROUP  BY Chromosome
),
loss_cnt AS (
  SELECT Chromosome,
         SUM(CAST(TotalCases AS INT64)) AS loss_cases
  FROM   `mitelman-db.prod.RecurrentNumData`
  WHERE  Morph = '3111' AND Topo = '0401'
    AND  STARTS_WITH(Abnormality,'-')
  GROUP  BY Chromosome
),
joined AS (
  SELECT g.Chromosome,
         g.gain_cases,
         l.loss_cases
  FROM   gain_cnt AS g
  JOIN   loss_cnt AS l USING (Chromosome)
  WHERE  g.gain_cases >= 5 AND l.loss_cases >= 5
)
SELECT CORR(gain_cases, loss_cases) AS pearson_corr,
       COUNT(*)                     AS chromosomes_compared
FROM   joined;
```

The queries above illustrate how to  
1) isolate the relevant breast‑cancer cohort,  
2) extract CNV (gain / loss) information,  
3) compute per‑chromosome frequencies, and  
4) perform an example Pearson correlation **within** Mitelman data.  

A very similar last step can be adapted to correlate the Mitelman frequencies with a TCGA frequency table once that table is available in BigQuery; the template merely needs the additional join to the TCGA frequencies and the same `CORR()` aggregate (plus an optional calculation of p‑values when desired).
